Predict 30-Day Hospital Readmission: Pipeline & Model Report
-Achintya Srikanth

1. Executive Summary

This report establishes a machine learning architecture designed to predict the risk of 30-day all-cause hospital readmissions. Using historical Electronic Health Record (EHR) and EMR registries from a hospital cohort, the framework isolates a complex subpopulation experiencing chronic multi-morbidities.

Core Specifications

    • Dataset Demographics: UCI Diabetes 130-US Hospitals (1999–2008) [1]

    • Cohort Scale Dimensions: 53,000 independent unique patient instances extracted from 68,000 baseline encounters.

    • Target Mapping Categories: Class 1 (<30 days early readmit), Class 2 (>30 days late readmit), Class 3 (NO readmission baseline).

    • Disease Conditions Selection:
        ? Diabetes Mellitus (ICD-9: 250)
        ? Chronic Kidney Disease / CKD (ICD-9: 585)
        ? Essential Hypertension / High Blood Pressure (ICD-9: 401)

    • Algorithmic Framework: Multi-Class Survival Stacking Ensemble (Super Learner Architecture) using a regularized meta-learner to merge tree-based survival representations.

    • Production Validation Performance: Harrell's Concordance Index (C-Index) of 0.7022, evaluated using patient-stratified 5-fold cross-validation.

2. Data Engineering & Preprocessing

The raw clinical database is structured by encounter entries, which present major pipeline challenges: data leakage, non-random missingness, high categorical cardinality, and informative right-censoring. The data engineering pipeline resolves these systemic issues prior to model injection.

Cohort Partitioning & Leakage Elimination:

    • Inclusion Filter: Patients with an active diagnosis code for Diabetes, CKD, or Hypertension present in any of the primary, secondary, or tertiary diagnostic fields (diag_1, diag_2, diag_3).

    • De-duplication Protocol: Standard longitudinal registries contain multiple temporal encounters per person (patient_nbr). Training models directly on multiple records violates the independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) data assumption. This creates artificial proximity, overfitting, and inflated accuracy.

    • Execution: The pipeline sorts records chronologically by encounter_id and drops all subsequent records per patient, isolating only the first index encounter. This reduces the initial 68,000 raw encounters down to an independent baseline matrix of 53,000 unique patients.

Survival Target Construct Formatting:

To avoid losing information by forcing the raw dataset classification classes (<30, >30, NO) into a basic binary framework, the target is modeled as an interval-censored survival tuple (T_i, delta_i) for each unique patient i:
    • Class 1: Readmitted within 30 days (<30): Mapped to an early midpoint proxy of (T_i = 15.0) days; event observed indicator (delta_i = 1.0).
    • Class 2: Readmitted after 30 days (>30): Mapped to a late subacute midpoint proxy of (T_i = 45.0) days; event observed indicator (delta_i = 1.0).
    • Class 3: Not readmitted (NO): Right-censored at the standard tracking limit boundary of (T_i = 60.0) days; event censored indicator (delta_i = 0.0).

Feature Matrix Transformations

    • Bracketed Variable Linearization: Categorical text age boundaries (e.g., [70-80)) are converted into their respective discrete numerical midpoints (e.g., 75). This preserves the underlying chronological ordinal relationships.
      
    • Variance Stabilization: Counts for historical healthcare usage (number_outpatient, number_emergency, number_inpatient) exhibit extreme right-skew distributions driven by super-utilizers. These features are smoothed using a log(x + 1) mathematical transformation to prevent extreme variances from skewing the tree split metrics.
      
    • Pharmacotherapy Titration Map: 23 distinct diabetic-specific medication columns are mapped from raw strings into an ordinal numeric scale: {'No': 0, 'Down': 1, 'Steady': 2, 'Up': 3}. This maps dosage changes directly to the tree split metrics.
      
    • ICD-9 Dimensionality Reduction: High-cardinality raw diagnostic code strings are grouped into collapsed clinical matrices matching major physical system categories (e.g., Circulatory, Respiratory, Digestive, Metabolic, Injury, Musculoskeletal, Genitourinary, Neoplasms, and External Causes). Missing values within low-density demographic fields are grouped into an explicit string token ("Unknown"), converting missing documentation into a predictive clinical signal.

3. Machine Learning Architecture: Survival Super Learner

The pipeline implements a dual-tier Stacking Ensemble (Super Learner framework). This approach leverages a diverse group of base models (Tier-0 Specialists) to extract risk features, which are then combined using an optimized linear-style survival meta-model (Tier-1 Blender).




























Tier-0 Base Models Setup

    • Model 1: Random Survival Forest (RSF): Constructs 100 non-parametric survival trees utilizing the log-rank splitting metric. It is designed to capture high-order, non-linear feature interactions without forcing predefined proportional hazard constraints.
      
    • Model 2: XGBoost Cox Proportional Hazards: Optimizes gradient-boosted tree ensembles by minimizing the partial log-likelihood function of a Cox model, handling sparse categorical indicators efficiently.
      
    • Model 3: XGBoost Accelerated Failure Time (AFT): Models the survival landscape using a parametric log-normal distribution. This provides a clean contrast to the proportional hazard models by estimating log-time acceleration rates directly.

Tier-1 Meta-Blender Optimization Strategy

To prevent data leakage and optimism bias during the blending stage, the Tier-1 coefficients are optimized using strictly Out-of-Fold (OOF) predictions. This matrix is generated across a Stratified 5-Fold Cross-Validation routine, ensuring that individual patient risks are predicted only by models trained on completely separate validation folds.

